# How Git must store and check out this repository's text, so that a clone made
# with the Git for Windows default (core.autocrlf=true) can still pass the
# checks CONTRIBUTING.md asks for. CI cannot catch a mistake here: every runner,
# the Windows ones included, checks out LF.
#
# Measured 2026-11-19 on this repository at 3f632e40, by cloning it with
# `git clone -c core.autocrlf=true` and running the gate against that clone.
# `go build`, `go vet` and the whole `go test ./...` suite (10 packages, 0
# failures) were unaffected; the two rules below are what broke.
#
# internal/cli/repo_line_endings_test.go asserts both rules over every file in
# each class, so a new file cannot quietly miss one.

# gofmt always writes LF, so it reports a CRLF file as unformatted on content
# nobody has touched. On the CRLF clone above, all 872 Go files checked out with
# CRLF and `gofmt -s -l .` listed all 872 -- the first command CONTRIBUTING.md
# tells a contributor to run, and a blocking CI check, cannot pass there at all.
*.go text eol=lf

# A script the kernel starts from its `#!` line cannot have a CR on that line:
# the CR becomes part of the interpreter's name. Measured here, a CRLF
# `#!/usr/bin/env python3` file exits 127 with
# `env: python3\r: No such file or directory`, and on the CRLF clone
# `sh scripts/entire-graph-statusline_test.sh` -- the POSIX shell suite in
# `mise run check` and in CI -- died at its second line with
# `: command not found` and `set: -: invalid option`, exit 2.
#
# The rules are globs but the guard is not: it reads the first two bytes of
# every tracked file and demands this attribute of anything beginning with
# `#!`, because the next script added here may not carry an extension at all.
# (Naming an interpreter explicitly, as CI does with
# `python3 scripts/entire-graph-prompt-context_test.py`, bypasses the `#!`
# line and hides the problem -- that suite's 41 tests passed on the CRLF
# clone.)
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.zsh text eol=lf

# Nothing else is pinned, and that is a finding rather than an omission. The
# NDJSON goldens are compared through normalizeSnapshotText
# (internal/sem/golden_test.go), which strips CR before the comparison, and the
# verify-block goldens hold hashes of freshly rendered output in JSON that is
# parsed rather than hashed, so neither depends on its file's line endings.
# docs/images/gh-repo-cover.png is the only committed file whose bytes contain
# a CR, and Git leaves it alone as binary. `git add --renormalize .` therefore
# rewrites nothing under these rules, which is the proof that they change
# checkout only and never content.
